{
  "doc_id": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE:11700821-DS-17:discharge_sections",
  "register": "notes",
  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nBurning in chest, cough\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ man with a PMH notable for HIV (last CD4 count in \n___ was 1068 and the corresponding viral load was \nundetectable) and hep C as well as paranoid schizophrenia and \nhemochromatosis presenting s/p an aspiration event. The patient \nswallowed ATRIPLA last night and reports it got stuck. He \nswallowed other meds without issues. He denies chest pain and \ndyspnea. Of note, the patient was drinking alcohol the entire \nday on ___. \n\nThis morning the patient was coughing up white phlegm and \npossibly small pill fragments. He expectorated a pill fragment \nthis morning. Chest burning started after woke up this morning \nand located on right side. Constant now improving. His pain \nworsens with cough. \n\nIn the ED, initial VS were 97.4 103 143/96 18 95% RA\nLabs were significant for: WBC 10.8, H/H 16.9/48.8, plt 167, Na \n139, K 3.9, Cl 104, HCO3 23, BUN 9, Cr 0.7, gluc 127 and AG 12.  \n\nCXR demonstrated new right lung lower lobe opacity compatible \nwith aspiration. CT scan showed: no focal consolidation or \npleural effusion. Interventional pulmonary was consulted and \nsaid that the patient has no current indication for bronch. \nReceived albuterol nebulizer, ceftriaxone 1G IV, oxycodone 5mg \nPO x2, lorazepam 2mg PO and benzonatate 200mg PO x1.\n\nPatient told ED staff he was afraid to go home because of his \ncough. He also said that he does not have a ride until the AM.  \n\nTransfer VS were 101 149/92 18 97% RA.\n\nOn arrival to the floor, patient reports residual burning \nsensation in his throat that is much improved. He is still \ncoughing and refuses to take Atripla tonight for fear of \nchoking. Otherwise he denies any pain or discomfort. He is \nasking to eat dinner.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ man with a PMH notable for HIV and hep C as well as \nparanoid schizophrenia and hemochromatosis presenting s/p an \naspiration event.\n\n#ASPIRATION: The patient's history is consistent with a small \nvolume aspiration including a foreign body (ATRIPLA pill). \nInitial CXR showed a RLL opacity potentially consistent with \naspiration. CT chest later during ED course showed no evidence \nof consolidation, or parenchymal involvement. Antibiotic was \ndiscontinued at that time. The patient's presentation was \nconsistent with chemical bronchitis not complicated by \naspiration pneumonia or pneumonitis. He was managed \nsymptomatically with nebulizers and antitussives with \nsignificant improvement in his symptoms by the time of \ndischarge.\n\n# HIV: last CD4 count in ___ was 1068 and the corresponding \nviral load was undetectable. Continued Atripla.\n\n# Paranoid schizophrenia: continued olanzapine, citalopram, \nclonazepam.\n\n# HTN: continue amlodipine, lisinopril.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary diagnosis:\n- Aspiration\n\nSecondary diagnosis:\n- Hypertension\n- HIV\n- Paranoid schizophrenia",
